{
  "id": "generics/class-template-substitution-member-type",
  "title": "A class-level template type argument substitutes into the member's return type",
  "domain": "generics",
  "criterion_id": "generics/class-template-substitution",
  "rule_class": "undefined-method",
  "php": { "min": "8.1" },
  "targets": ["src"],
  "ground_truth": {
    "basis": "runtime_proof",
    "statement": "Box declares @template T with @return T on get(), so the annotation @param Box<Cat> tells the caller that get() hands back a Cat, and the member access that follows has to be resolved against Cat - which has no bark(). The runtime script decides both halves independently of any analyzer: a value taken from a Box<Dog> dispatches Dog::bark() and returns 'woof', a value taken from a Box<Cat> dispatches Cat::meow() and returns 'meow' (so the substituted type is the class the annotation names, not mixed), and the same get()->bark() access on the Box<Cat> side fails with 'Error: Call to undefined method Cat::bark()'. Callers cannot weaken this: the class-level template is part of the annotation the callee published, exactly like a native parameter type.",
    "runtime_script": "runtime/check.php",
    "citation": "https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.error.php"
  },
  "expect": {
    "phpstan": {
      "mode": "diagnostics",
      "required": [
        { "code": "method.notFound", "line": 47, "message_regex": "Cat::bark" }
      ],
      "forbidden": [
        { "code": "method.notFound", "line": 41 },
        { "code": "method.notFound", "line": 53 }
      ]
    },
    "psalm": {
      "mode": "diagnostics",
      "required": [
        { "code": "UndefinedMethod", "line": 47, "message_regex": "Cat::bark" }
      ],
      "forbidden": [
        { "code": "UndefinedMethod", "line": 41 },
        { "code": "UndefinedMethod", "line": 53 }
      ]
    },
    "mago": {
      "mode": "diagnostics",
      "required": [
        { "code": "non-existent-method", "line": 47, "message_regex": "bark.*Cat" }
      ],
      "forbidden": [
        { "code": "non-existent-method", "line": 41 },
        { "code": "non-existent-method", "line": 53 }
      ]
    }
  },
  "notes": "The rule under test is template substitution into a member's type at a use site: the same member access, on the same generic class, with only the type argument changed. All three tools substitute and report, under their own code: PHPStan 2.3.1 method.notFound, Psalm 6.20.0 UndefinedMethod, Mago 1.57.0 non-existent-method. The two forbidden predicates are the asymmetric controls and they are what makes the row about substitution rather than about member access: line 41 is the identical get()->bark() on Box<Dog>, which must stay silent, and line 53 is get()->meow() on Box<Cat>, which must stay silent too. The second control is the harder one - a tool that treated the declared @return mixed of get() as the receiver would report meow() as undefined on mixed and fail the case - so a tool cannot pass by guessing. The required predicates also pin the substituted class in the message ('Cat::bark' / 'bark ... Cat'), so an undefined-method report that does not name Cat does not satisfy the case. Every diagnostic the three tools produced is claimed by a predicate; nothing is acknowledged away and nothing is widened. No existing case in this corpus uses an undefined-method rule class, and none of the other generics cases covers member resolution on a substituted type argument (they cover inference from an argument and a declared bound). The round's source material, Mago's analyzer test suite, is a directory of fixtures without catalogue candidate IDs, and no catalogue lead covers this rule, so no source_candidate_id is recorded.",
  "provenance": {
    "sources": [
      {
        "kind": "upstream_test_case",
        "path": "/home/abez/projects/mago-upstream-prep/mago/crates/analyzer/tests/cases/generics_template_chained_call_wrong_consumer.php",
