{
  "id": "generics/covariant-template-in-write-position",
  "title": "A covariant template used in a parameter position makes the declared variance unsound",
  "domain": "generics",
  "criterion_id": "generics/template-variance",
  "rule_class": "template-variance-violation",
  "php": { "min": "8.1" },
  "targets": ["src"],
  "ground_truth": {
    "basis": "runtime_proof",
    "statement": "Bag declares @template-covariant T, which promises that a Bag<Dog> may be used wherever a Bag<Animal> is expected, and it also declares push() with @param T, a write position. Those two promises cannot both hold. The runtime script executes the program the declarations license: put_cat() is typed Bag<Animal>, the covariant promise makes the caller's Bag<Dog> acceptable to it, put_cat() stores a Cat in that box, and the caller - whose annotation says Bag<Dog> - reads a Cat back. A Cat is not a Dog (the script checks the sibling relationship directly), and adopt() declares the native return type Dog, so the call fails with 'adopt(): Return value must be of type Dog, Cat returned'. A fresh Bag constructed with a Dog does read back a Dog, so the failure is caused by the declared write position and not by the widening on its own.",
